{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  // The scheduled Worker: two jobs, two schedules, one deploy.
  //
  //   npx wrangler deploy -c wrangler.cron.jsonc
  //   npx wrangler secret bulk secret.json -c wrangler.cron.jsonc
  //   npx wrangler dev -c wrangler.cron.jsonc --test-scheduled
  //
  // **Why it is separate from the application Worker** is recorded in full at
  // the top of `worker/scheduled.ts`, and it comes down to one measurement: a
  // `scheduled()` handler added to the app's Worker has to import application
  // code, wrangler's esbuild pass compiles that separately from the copy
  // OpenNext already bundled, and the Prisma query compiler shipped twice —
  // 3812 KiB gzip against what was then a 3072 KiB ceiling.
  //
  // T51 has since closed (Workers Paid, 10 MiB) and the split stands anyway, on
  // T51's own closing note: shipping the query compiler twice was a
  // correctness-of-bundling problem, not a size-limit one.
  "name": "reserva-barber-cron",
  "main": "worker/scheduled.ts",
  // Kept identical to the app's Worker on purpose: both run the same Prisma
  // client against the same database, and a divergence in runtime behaviour
  // between them would be invisible until one of them misbehaved.
  "compatibility_date": "2025-09-01",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },
  // Non-secret, and here rather than in the hosting dashboard for the reason
  // `wrangler.jsonc` gives about its own: a value kept only in the dashboard is
  // a value the next deploy from a fresh clone silently lacks.
  //
  // **`APP_ORIGIN` is new in N2, and this file previously stated that this
  // Worker had none.** That was true and load-bearing while the only job was
  // the sweep, which renders nothing and composes no URL. The reminder sends a
  // message whose entire point is a link, so the statement is corrected here
  // rather than left standing — a justification a change has falsified is a
  // sentence the next reader will trust.
  //
  // Its absence is handled, not fatal: the reminder still sends, omits the
  // link, and logs once per run. That degradation costs more here than on the
  // confirmation path — a confirmation without a link is still a receipt, while
  // a reminder without one has had its purpose removed.
  //
  // `EMAIL_FROM` is **deliberately absent** until a sending domain is verified
  // (T76). A provider's shared onboarding sender delivers only to the account
  // owner's own address, so it would pass a verification done from that inbox
  // and silently drop every real client. Its intended value is
  //
  //   "EMAIL_FROM": "Reserva Barber <turnos@your-verified-domain>"
  //
  // documented here rather than guessed, per the rule `wrangler.jsonc` and the
  // booking-confirmation-email capability both state.
  "vars": {
    "APP_ORIGIN": "https://reserva-barber.franco-galeano.workers.dev"
  },
  // **Two schedules, dispatched on `event.cron` in `worker/scheduled.ts`.**
  //
  // Not two calls in one handler body. Each job rethrows on failure, because
  // that is what marks the invocation failed in the platform's own view of the
  // schedule and a dead job that looks healthy is the failure this whole family
  // of jobs is written against. Sharing a handler would mean either the
  // reminder's fault marking a healthy sweep failed, or the sweep's rethrow
  // stopping the reminder ever reporting.
  //
  //   "*/5 * * * *"  the expired-hold sweep (B7)
  //
  // Five minutes is a data-freshness choice, not a correctness one: a lapsed
  // hold stopped blocking its slot the instant it lapsed, because availability
  // reads `holdExpiresAt` rather than the status. Every minute would spend
  // twelve times the connections against a pooler capped at five and shared
  // with the dashboard and the public booking write.
  //
  //   "0 * * * *"    the appointment reminder (N2)
  //
  // Hourly, and also a data-freshness choice: at a 24-hour lead nothing anybody
  // can see depends on the message landing within five minutes. **The
  // reminder's correctness does not depend on this value** — its candidate
  // window ends at the appointment rather than being centred on a target
  // instant, so anything a missed run skipped is still a candidate on the next
  // one. The cadence can be changed without touching a rule, a query or an
  // index.
  //
  // **Removing a schedule is the rollback for its job.**
  //
  // For B7 that un-expires nothing and does not need to: a swept row and an
  // unswept lapsed row are indistinguishable to availability, to the booking
  // write and to the confirmation page. The only path that can tell them apart
  // is a late Mercado Pago approval, which is what `EXPIRY_GRACE_MINUTES`
  // protects.
  //
  // For N2 it stops reminders and un-claims nothing, deliberately: a claimed
  // row may already have been delivered, and releasing it is how a bounded loss
  // becomes an unbounded duplicate. The column and its index are inert without
  // the schedule.
  // **The reminder's schedule is deliberately NOT listed yet.**
  //
  // Adding it is the only irreversible step in shipping N2, and it is one line.
  // Until it is here the reminder job is unreachable code on a Worker nobody
  // can call — which is what makes the deploy, the bundle measurement and the
  // live gate verifiable in isolation, on the real runtime, with no chance of a
  // message reaching anybody.
  //
  // The order the story ships in:
  //
  //   1. deploy with this array unchanged   (proves the bundle)
  //   2. set APP_ORIGIN and EMAIL_FROM      (RESEND_API_KEY stays unset, T76)
  //   3. add "0 * * * *" here and deploy    (the one irreversible line)
  "triggers": {
    "crons": ["*/5 * * * *"]
  }
  // `DATABASE_URL` and `RESEND_API_KEY` are secrets and are NOT defined here.
  // They are set separately from the app Worker's — same values, two Workers,
  // two uploads. There is no shared secret store between Workers, and a
  // credential present on one is absent on the other with no symptom other than
  // the feature that needs it failing.
  //
  // **`RESEND_API_KEY` must stay UNSET on this Worker in production while T76
  // stands.** No sending domain is verified, so a key here would make the job
  // appear to send while reaching exactly one mailbox.
  //
  // Unset is a **handled state, and it had to be made one.** Because the
  // reminder claims each row *before* sending, an unconfigured deployment would
  // otherwise claim every due booking and deliver nothing — permanently, on the
  // first run. An earlier version of this comment claimed the job "claims
  // nothing it cannot serve", which was simply false; the adversarial pass
  // caught it before deploy. `runReminders` now checks the configuration
  // BEFORE it queries anything: it logs the missing variables once, emits a
  // zero summary so a refusal is not silence, and returns without touching the
  // database. It does not mark the invocation failed — this is the state the
  // deployment was told to be in.
  //
  // Upload as EXACT BYTES, piped from a file or a program's output, never
  // through a shell echo: a byte-order mark or a trailing newline is invisible
  // and surfaces as an unrelated connection error.
  //
  // **Use `secret bulk` with a JSON file. The interactive prompt cost an hour
  // here.** B7's first two production runs, twenty minutes apart, both threw
  // `Missing required environment variable: DATABASE_URL` while
  // `wrangler secret list` reported the secret present — because `list` shows
  // the name and never the value, and the guard that failed reads `!value`,
  // which an empty string satisfies just as `undefined` does. The same
  // 112-character string, uploaded from a JSON file instead of typed at the
  // prompt, worked on the very next run.
  //
  //   npx wrangler secret bulk secret.json -c wrangler.cron.jsonc
  //
  // Write that file with something that emits UTF-8 without a BOM — PowerShell
  // 5.1's `Set-Content -Encoding utf8` does not — and delete it afterwards.
  // JSON is what makes it safe: the value is delimited by quotes, so trailing
  // whitespace and partial pastes cannot pass unnoticed.
  //
  // There is still no `ASSETS` binding and **no `fetch` handler at all**, which
  // is the cheapest possible answer to "can a stranger reach these jobs".
}
